'''''Lycium''''' is a [[genus]] of flowering plants in the [[Solanum|nightshade]] [[Family (taxonomy)|family]], [[Solanaceae]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uniprot.org/taxonomy/24646 |title=Genus ''Lycium'' |work=Taxonomy |publisher=[[UniProt]] |access-date=16 April 2009 }}</ref> The genus has a [[disjunct distribution]] around the globe, with species occurring on most continents in [[temperate climate|temperate]] and [[subtropics|subtropical]] regions. South America has the most species, followed by North America and southern Africa. There are several scattered across Europe and Asia, and one is native to Australia.<ref name=fu>Fukuda, T., et al. (2001). [http://www2.uah.es/mcee/Lycium-Solanaceae.pdf Phylogeny and biogeography of the genus ''Lycium'' (Solanaceae): Inferences from chloroplast DNA sequences.] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20031130213906/http://www2.uah.es/mcee/Lycium-Solanaceae.pdf |date=30 November 2003 }} ''Molecular Phylogenetics and Evolution'' 19(2), 246-58.</ref> Common English names for plants of this genus include '''box-thorn''',<ref name=jeps>[http://ucjeps.berkeley.edu/cgi-bin/get_IJM.pl?tid=11452 ''Lycium''.] The Jepson eFlora 2013.</ref> '''[[goji|wolfberry]]''', and '''desert-thorn'''.<ref name=itis>{{cite web |url=https://www.itis.gov/servlet/SingleRpt/SingleRpt?search_topic=TSN&search_value=30531 |title=Lycium |publisher=Integrated Taxonomic Information System }}</ref> [[Plants of the World Online]] currently accepts 101 species.<ref name = powo/> Other estimates are of 70<ref name=fu/> to 80<ref name=china>[http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=2&taxon_id=119146 ''Lycium''.] Flora of China.</ref><ref name=levin>Levin, R. A. and J. S. Miller. (2005). [https://web.archive.org/web/20161226004140/http://www.amjbot.org/content/92/12/2044.full Relationships within tribe Lycieae (Solanaceae): paraphyly of ''Lycium'' and multiple origins of gender dimorphism.] ''American Journal of Botany'' 92(12), 2044-53.</ref> species.

==Etymology==

The generic name ''Lycium'' is derived from the Greek word λυκιον (''lykion''), which was applied by [[Pliny the Elder]] (23-79) and [[Pedanius Dioscorides]] (ca. 40–90) to a plant known as dyer's buckthorn. It was probably a ''[[Rhamnus (plant)|Rhamnus]]'' species and was named for [[Lycia]] (Λυκία), the ancient southern [[Anatolia]]n region in which it grew.<ref name="Austin">{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=eS7lX_rC3GEC |first=D. F. |last=Austin |title=Florida Ethnobotany |publisher=CRC Press |year=2004 |isbn=9780849323324 |pages=677 }}</ref><ref name="rhs">Huxley, A., ed. (1992). ''New RHS Dictionary of Gardening''. Macmillan {{ISBN|0-333-47494-5}}.</ref> The berry is called ''lycii fructus'' ("lycium fruit") in old [[Latin]] [[pharmacology|pharmacological]] texts.

==Description==
[[Image:LyciumBarbarum-bloem-hr.jpg|thumb|left|''[[Lycium barbarum]]'']]
''Lycium'' are [[shrub]]s, often [[Thorns, spines, and prickles|thorny]], growing 1 to 4 meters tall. The leaves are small, narrow, and fleshy, and are alternately arranged, sometimes in [[Fascicle (botany)|fascicles]]. Flowers are solitary or borne in clusters. The funnel-shaped or bell-shaped corolla is white, green, or purple in color. The fruit is a two-chambered, usually fleshy and juicy [[Berry (botany)|berry]] which can be red, orange, yellow, or black. It may have few seeds or many.<ref name=jeps/><ref name=china/> Most ''Lycium'' have fleshy, red berries with over 10 seeds, but a few American taxa have hard fruits with two seeds.<ref name=levin/>

While most ''Lycium'' are [[Plant reproductive morphology|monoecious]], producing bisexual flowers with functional male and female parts, some species are [[dioecy|gynodioecious]], with some individuals bearing bisexual flowers and some producing functionally female flowers.<ref>Miller, J. S. and D. L. Venable. (2002). {{Deprecated archive|url=https://archive.today/20130903050432/http://www.amjbot.org/content/89/12/1907.full|title=The transition to gender dimorphism on an evolutionary background of self-incompatibility: an example from ''Lycium'' (Solanaceae).}} ''American Journal of Botany'' 89(12), 1907-15.</ref>

==Uses==
''Lycium'' has been known to European [[Herbalism|herbalists]] since ancient times, and species were traded from the Far East to Europe [[Roman trade with India|by the Romans]], for example via [[Ariaca]] and the port of [[Barbarikon]] near today's [[Karachi]], as mentioned in the ''[[Periplus of the Erythraean Sea]]''. In his ''[[Natural History (Pliny)|Naturalis historia]]'', [[Pliny the Elder]] describes boxthorn as a medicinal plant, as does [[Pedanius Dioscorides]] in his ''P. Dioscoridae pharmacorum simplicium reique medicae''.<ref name=hitch/>

In his 1753 publication ''[[Species Plantarum]]'', [[Carl Linnaeus|Linnaeus]] describes three ''Lycium'' species: ''[[Lycium afrum|L. afrum]]'', ''[[Lycium barbarum|L. barbarum]]'', and ''[[Lycium europaeum|L. europaeum]]''.<ref name=hitch>Hitchcock, C. L. (1932). A monographic study of the genus ''Lycium'' of the Western Hemisphere. ''Annals of the Missouri Botanical Garden'' 19(2/3), 179-348 and 350-66. doi:10.2307/2394155 (First page image).</ref>

[[Image:Lycium-barbarum-fruits.JPG|thumb|right|''L. barbarum'']]
''Lycium'', particularly ''[[Lycium barbarum|L. barbarum]]'', have long been used in [[traditional Chinese medicine]].<ref name="Gross">{{cite web |url=https://www.newhope.com/natural-product-trends/food-and-beverage |title=Goji: what it is... and isn't |author=Gross PM |year=2007 |publisher=NewHope Network, Penton Media Inc. }}</ref><ref name="UK-FSA">{{cite web |url=http://www.food.gov.uk/multimedia/pdfs/gojiberriesrep.pdf |title=Goji Berries |date=June 2007 |publisher=UK Food Standards Agency, Novel Foods, Additives and Supplements Division |access-date=13 April 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121120203800/http://www.food.gov.uk/multimedia/pdfs/gojiberriesrep.pdf |archive-date=20 November 2012 }}</ref> The leaves and roots of other species of ''Lycium'', such as ''L. europaeum'', when mixed with water, have been used in folk medicine.<ref>Nissim Krispil, ''Medicinal Plants in Israel and Throughout the World - the Complete Guide'', Or Yehuda (Israel) 2000, pp.{{nbsp}}38–39 (Hebrew)</ref> The fruit of ''L. barbarum'' and ''L. chinense'', known as [[goji]] berry, is commonly consumed as a dried fruit.<ref name=Gross/> The Chinese tonic ''gou qi zi'' ("wolfberry fruit") is made of the fruit of any of several ''Lycium'' species, and is used as a [[dietary supplement]].<ref name=Gross/>

==Ecology==
''Lycium'' species mostly occur in [[desert climate|arid]] and semi-arid climates, and a few are known from coastal zones in somewhat saline habitat types.<ref name=fu/>

[[Invasive species]] include ''[[Lycium ferocissimum|L. ferocissimum]]'', which was introduced to Australia and New Zealand and has become a dense, thorny pest plant there. It injures livestock, harbors pest mammals and insects, and displaces native species.<ref>[http://www.cabi.org/isc/?compid=5&dsid=31903&loadmodule=datasheet&page=481&site=144 ''Lycium ferocissimum'' (African boxthorn).] Invasive Species Compendium. CABI.</ref>

